@node emacsctl over SSH
@section emacsctl over SSH

@example
emacsctl --host user@@machine instances
emacsctl --host user@@machine logs -f      # live streaming works
emacsctl --host user@@machine repl --lang bacon
@end example

Requirements on the @emph{remote} host: a running cmacs, and
@command{emacsctl} on the non-interactive ssh @env{PATH} (set
@env{EMACSCTL_REMOTE_COMMAND} locally to an absolute remote path if
it lives elsewhere).

@option{--host} spawns @code{ssh -o BatchMode=yes -- HOST emacsctl
proxy [--instance N]}.  The remote @code{emacsctl proxy} (a hidden
command) bridges its stdin/stdout to the @emph{remote} session
D-Bus.  Both ends speak a length-prefixed JSON frame protocol over
the ssh pipes (4-byte big-endian length + JSON object): @code{hello}
handshake, id-correlated @code{call}/@code{reply}/@code{error}
frames, and @code{subscribe}/@code{signal} frames for live
streaming.  GVariant payloads cross as JSON via json-glib's
signature-directed @code{json_gvariant_serialize} /
@code{deserialize}.

Signal frames interleave with replies on one full-duplex stream,
which is what makes @code{logs -f}, @code{watch stream} and REPLs
work remotely.  Authentication and encryption are plain ssh; the
proxy listens on nothing --- it only bridges the stdio ssh gave it.

Troubleshooting: ``failed before handshake'' means the remote
command didn't start (try @code{ssh HOST emacsctl --version});
``proxy protocol mismatch'' means the remote binary is a different
protocol generation; @code{BatchMode=yes} means no password prompts,
so set up keys.
